---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

// Exported so /llms.txt can enumerate this page without a second copy of its
// title and description. A new page with a `meta` export appears there for free.
//
// Register (infra/docs/design/gtm-dual-icp.md): ICP-2-facing — the
// commitment-ladder argument. Never "abandon your tools"; each rung pays for
// itself and abandonment is a conclusion the reader reaches.
export const meta = {
  order: 7,
  label: 'The ladder',
  title: 'Why the data moves in · Beadhive',
  description:
    'Agents can\'t plan against data they can\'t query. The commitment ladder: sync the ' +
    'tracker, move the docs, record the reviews, hook the telemetry — each rung pays off on ' +
    'its own, and retiring the old tools is a conclusion you reach, not a demand we make.',
};
---
<Layout active="data" title={meta.title} description={meta.description}>
<div class="pagegrid">
  <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <p class="toc-title">On this page</p>
    <a class="head" href="#principle">The principle</a>
    <a class="head" href="#rungs">The rungs</a>
    <a class="head" href="#conclusion">Nothing is abandoned first</a>
    <a class="head" href="#top">Where the ladder tops out</a>
  </nav>
  <div class="pagemain">

<section id="principle">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow display">09 · The ladder</div>
    <h2 class="display">Agents can't plan against data they can't query</h2>
    <p class="lede-sm">You've consolidated systems before and regretted some of them. This page
    is the argument for why this consolidation is different — one rung at a time, each rung
    paying for itself.</p>
    <p>Right now your lifecycle is scattered by design: issues in one SaaS, decisions in a docs
    folder or someone's Drive, review reasoning in PR threads, incident knowledge in dashboards
    and postmortem docs. A senior engineer holds the connections between them in their head.
    An agent can't — and neither can the next engineer.</p>
    <p>Files are readable but not <em>queryable</em>. An agent can open your design doc; it
    cannot ask what's blocked by it, what depends on it, or whether the incident from March
    already invalidated it. Planning against unqueryable data is improvisation with extra
    steps — which is exactly the failure you've watched AI coding tools repeat. The fix is not
    a smarter agent. It's putting the lifecycle where every seat can ask it questions: the
    work-graph.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="rungs">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow display">One at a time</div>
    <h2 class="display">The rungs — each pays for itself</h2>
    {/* The ladder drawn as a ladder: each rung carries a numbered comb-cell
        badge on its top edge, and a rail threads badge to badge — a straight
        drop when the cards stack, a stepped descent through the staggered 2×2
        on a laptop. The rail is pseudo-element chrome on .rung; the badge is
        real content so the rung numbers survive for screen readers. */}
    <div class="cardgrid runggrid">
      <div class="rung"><div class="card"><h4><span class="rungno"><svg viewBox="0 0 30 34" aria-hidden="true"><polygon points="15,2 28,9.5 28,24.5 15,32 2,24.5 2,9.5"/></svg><span>1</span></span> Sync the issue tracker</h4>
        <p>Bi-directional sync; beads becomes the working copy, your tracker keeps running.</p>
        <p><b>Payoff:</b> "what's next" is <em>computed</em> from real dependency edges, and
        the plan survives any agent losing its context.</p></div></div>
      <div class="rung"><div class="card"><h4><span class="rungno"><svg viewBox="0 0 30 34" aria-hidden="true"><polygon points="15,2 28,9.5 28,24.5 15,32 2,24.5 2,9.5"/></svg><span>2</span></span> Move the design docs in</h4>
        <p>Backfill proposes your ADRs, specs and git history as beads — reconciled, never
        blind-imported.</p>
        <p><b>Payoff:</b> planning cites recorded decisions instead of re-litigating them, and
        a decision has an address other work can depend on.</p></div></div>
      <div class="rung"><div class="card"><h4><span class="rungno"><svg viewBox="0 0 30 34" aria-hidden="true"><polygon points="15,2 28,9.5 28,24.5 15,32 2,24.5 2,9.5"/></svg><span>3</span></span> Record reviews in the graph</h4>
        <p>Review verdicts become gates — records, not vibes in a chat thread — signed by
        whoever held the seat.</p>
        <p><b>Payoff:</b> a bounce targets the process that produced the defect, not just the
        diff, so the correction sticks.</p></div></div>
      <div class="rung"><div class="card"><h4><span class="rungno"><svg viewBox="0 0 30 34" aria-hidden="true"><polygon points="15,2 28,9.5 28,24.5 15,32 2,24.5 2,9.5"/></svg><span>4</span></span> Hook up the telemetry</h4>
        <p>Production signal joins the same graph the planner reads.</p>
        <p><b>Payoff:</b> an incident doesn't end at mitigation — it becomes a guardrail in
        the next plan, the loop everything else on this site exists to close.</p></div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="conclusion">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow display">No leap required</div>
    <h2 class="display">Nothing is abandoned first</h2>
    <p>Rung one is a sync, not a migration. Your tracker, your docs, your dashboards all keep
    working; beads is simply the copy the agents plan against. What happens next is the same
    thing that happens to any mirrored system: one copy is where the decisions get made and
    the other goes quietly stale. When you retire the old tool — if you do — it's because
    nobody had opened it in a month, not because a landing page told you to.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="top">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow display">The view from above</div>
    <h2 class="display">Where the ladder tops out</h2>
    <p>With the whole lifecycle in one queryable graph, the seats can run without you in the
    loop of every step: planners file gated plans, developers work headless in their own
    worktrees, reviewers and wardens hold their gates, and production reports back into the
    next cycle. Your seat rises to where a CTO's should be — reading the fleet at a glance,
    approving at the gates that matter, steering by what the graph says instead of by reading
    everything yourself.</p>
    <p><a class="deeplink" href="/roles/">The seats and who holds what</a><br/>
    <a class="deeplink" href="/fleet/">The fleet view — every hive from one seat</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

  </div>
</div>
</Layout>

<style>
  /* ---------- the ladder rail ----------
     Below 1024px the rungs stack single-column and the rail is a straight
     drop: each badge hangs from the card above it. The thread's grammar is
     "rail passes behind the cards, surfaces in the gaps" — segments emerge
     from a card edge and dive into the next rung's badge. */
  .runggrid { position: relative; grid-template-columns: 1fr; row-gap: 30px; }
  .rung { position: relative; }
  .rung > .card { height: 100%; }
  /* Setup line above, payoff line below — the gap is what makes the split scan. */
  .rung > .card p + p { margin-top: 9px; }

  /* Numbered comb-cell badge straddling each rung's top edge. Real content,
     not chrome: the number is what orders the ladder. */
  .rungno {
    position: absolute; top: -17px; left: 22px; width: 30px; height: 34px;
    display: grid; place-items: center;
  }
  .rungno svg { position: absolute; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; }
  .rungno polygon { fill: var(--honey-wash); stroke: var(--honey); stroke-width: 2; }
  .rungno > span {
    position: relative; line-height: 1;
    font-family: "Futura", "Avenir Next", system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 15px; font-weight: 800; color: var(--honey-strong);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  /* straight drop: previous card's underside → this rung's badge */
  .rung + .rung::before {
    content: ""; position: absolute;
    left: 36px; top: -30px; width: 2px; height: 13px;
    background: var(--mark-line); opacity: .4;
  }

  /* ≥1024px: a true 2×2 with room to breathe — and the right column staggered
     down, so 1→2→3→4 also reads top-to-bottom by eye. The rail becomes a
     stepped descent, drawn with border elbows in the grid gaps. */
  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    #rungs > .wrap { max-width: 68rem; }
    .runggrid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
      gap: 2rem 2.5rem;
    }
    .rung:nth-child(even) { margin-top: 2.75rem; }
    .rung > .card { padding: 26px 30px; }
    .rung > .card h4 { font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 10px; }
    .rung > .card p { font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.62; }
    .rungno { left: 26px; }

    /* 1→2 and 3→4: out of the left card's right edge, elbow down into the
       staggered right card's badge. (`.rung + .rung` repeated here so these
       outrank the base stub rule once Astro's scoping attributes are added.) */
    .rung + .rung:nth-child(even)::before {
      left: -2.5rem; top: -38px;
      width: calc(2.5rem + 38px); height: 21px;
      background: none;
      border-top: 2px solid var(--mark-line);
      border-right: 2px solid var(--mark-line);
      border-top-right-radius: 12px;
    }
    /* 2→3: down from under the right card, back across the row gap, into the
       left card's badge from the side. */
    .rung + .rung:nth-child(3)::before {
      left: 56px; top: -32px;
      width: calc(100% + 2.5rem - 19px); height: 18px;
      background: none;
      border-right: 2px solid var(--mark-line);
      border-bottom: 2px solid var(--mark-line);
      border-bottom-right-radius: 12px;
    }
  }
</style>
